export default "## Why Cycle Data Helps in Gynecologist Appointments\n\nYour clinician may ask about cycle dates, bleeding, pain, and repeat symptoms. A short summary can make that easier to discuss. The hard part is having those notes in a usable form when you arrive.\n\n\"My periods are pretty heavy\" is less useful to a clinician than \"my periods last six days, I go through four to five heavy pads on days two and three, and I've had this pattern for at least six months.\" The first is a description. The second is data.\n\nFloriva's on-device logging helps you build the second version while core tracking stays on your device.\n\n## What Consistency Actually Means\n\nConsistency in cycle logging doesn't mean logging every possible field every day. It means logging the most important fields on the days they're relevant, for every cycle.\n\nIf it helps, you can note period start date, period end date, flow, pain, and spotting. Bring what you have.\n\nSeveral cycles of simple notes can be easier to discuss than a long record with many gaps.\n\n## The Privacy Advantage for Appointment Notes\n\nWhen you build appointment notes in Floriva, core tracking stays on your device. You decide what to bring to the appointment. Screenshots, exports, backups, shared phones, and messages can still create copies.\n\n[Period tracker data is not always covered by HIPAA](/resources/guides/period-tracker-hipaa/). Data you share from cloud-based apps may be governed by that app's privacy policy, not medical privacy law. If you share Floriva data, a summary you choose from on-device records can limit extra detail.\n\nFor condition-specific notes, see the [endometriosis tracking guide](/app-guides/floriva-for-endometriosis-tracking/) and the [PCOS tracking guide](/app-guides/floriva-for-pcos-tracking/).\n\nFor cycle-linked migraine notes, use the [menstrual migraine appointment prep checklist](/free/menstrual-migraine-log) or the [menstrual migraine cycle log](/free/menstrual-migraine-log).\n\nFor urinary symptoms, use the [UTI symptom tracker](/free/uti-urinary-symptom-tracker), [UTI after sex visit prep checklist](/free/uti-urinary-symptom-tracker), or [recurrent UTI appointment prep checklist](/free/uti-urinary-symptom-tracker) before you make a one-page summary.\n\nFor bowel symptoms, use the [period symptom tracker](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker), the [period cramp and pain diary](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary), or the [digestive cycle data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data) before you decide what to bring.\n\nFor pain that moves or is hard to place, use the [period pain location visit summary](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary) or [hip pain during period log](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary). If you only want to share the smallest useful copy, use the [period pain location data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data) first.\n\nFor bleeding after sex or sex pain notes, use the [bleeding after sex tracker](/free/abnormal-bleeding-log), [pain during sex symptom notes](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary), or [sex pain and bleeding data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data).\n\nFor vulvar itching, burning, rash, swelling, dryness, or product timing notes, use the [vulvar itching symptom log](/free/vulvar-symptom-log), [vulvar burning symptom notes](/free/vulvar-symptom-log), [vulvar rash skin change notes](/free/vulvar-symptom-log), [vulvar irritation product exposure log](/free/vulvar-symptom-log), [labia swelling visit notes](/free/vulvar-symptom-log), or [vaginal dryness before period notes](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker).\n\nFor period product notes, use the [period product irritation log](/free/period-leak-and-product-kit), [pad rash symptom log](/free/period-leak-and-product-kit), [tampon pain visit notes](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary), [menstrual cup pain notes](/free/period-pain-cramp-diary), or [period product symptom data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data).\n\nFor feverish or sick feelings near a period, use the [period flu symptom tracker](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker), [fever and period symptom notes](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker), or [period flu data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data).\n\nFor PMS body and mood notes, use the [breast tenderness before period tracker](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker), [period cravings tracker](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker), [acne before period skin log](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker), [anger before period notes](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker), or [PMS body data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data).\n\nFor sleep and low-energy notes, use the [period sleep disruption visit prep](/free/period-sleep-log), [sleep and period symptom summary](/free/period-sleep-log), [fatigue before period tracker](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker), or [sleep cycle data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data).\n\nFor focus, memory, or work and school impact notes, use the [cycle focus visit summary](/free/reproductive-visit-prep-kit) or the [focus mood cycle data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data) before you decide what to bring.";
